<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  OWASP Dependency-Check suppressions for the backend's dependency tree.

  Empty by design: nothing is suppressed yet because the scan has not
  produced a reviewed finding to suppress. This file exists so that the day
  one does, the mechanism and its required shape are already in place and
  reviewable in a diff, rather than invented under pressure alongside a real
  CVE.

  Every <suppress> entry added here must carry, inside <notes>, all four of:
    - the CVE (or vulnerabilityName) being suppressed
    - why it does not apply to this codebase (unreachable code path, fixed
      upstream in a way the CVE database has not caught up with, etc.)
    - the person or team who reviewed and approved the suppression
    - the review date

  and must set the `until` attribute to an explicit expiry date
  (yyyy-MM-dd, or yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ss.SSSZ). Dependency-Check itself stops
  honouring a suppression once `until` has passed — an expired entry reverts
  to "not suppressed", so a CVE that is still unpatched starts failing the
  build again automatically rather than staying silenced by an entry nobody
  revisited. Suppressions without an `until` date, or without all four notes
  above, must not be merged.

  Example (do not uncomment without a real, reviewed finding):

  <suppress until="2026-12-31">
     <notes>
        CVE-2026-XXXXX: reported against transitive dependency X, but the
        vulnerable code path (Y) is never invoked by this application —
        Z calls only the unaffected W entry point. Reviewed by
        <owner/team>, <yyyy-mm-dd>. Re-review before expiry.
     </notes>
     <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/com\.example/vulnerable-lib@.*$</packageUrl>
     <cve>CVE-2026-XXXXX</cve>
  </suppress>

  See: https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/general/suppression.html
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">
   <!--
     CVE-2026-66299 — Apache Tomcat, low-severity DoS in the *WebSocket chat
     example application* bundled with the full Tomcat distribution
     (webapps/examples/websocket/chat): an unbounded per-client buffer for
     undelivered messages lets a slow client exhaust memory. Fixed upstream
     in Tomcat 10.1.58, which is not yet released as of the review date
     below (Maven Central: only 10.1.57 and a subsequent 10.1.59 exist;
     10.1.58 itself is absent, matching Apache's own security page marking
     it "not yet released").

     Verified inapplicable to this application:
       1. The embedded Tomcat JARs this application actually resolves
          (tomcat-embed-core, tomcat-embed-websocket, tomcat-embed-el, all
          10.1.57) were inspected directly (`unzip -l`) and contain zero
          entries matching "example" or "chat" in any path. The vulnerable
          webapp ships only inside the full apache-tomcat-*.zip/tar.gz
          server distribution's webapps/examples directory — a separate
          artifact never pulled in by Spring Boot's embedded-Tomcat starter.
       2. apps/backend/Dockerfile packages this application as a single
          Spring Boot executable jar (`mvn package`, packaging type jar) and
          runs it with `java -jar`; there is no standalone Tomcat
          installation, no webapps/ directory, and nothing here deploys or
          could deploy Tomcat's own examples webapp.
       3. This codebase has no WebSocket endpoints of any kind (no
          @ServerEndpoint, WebSocketConfigurer, or @EnableWebSocket usage
          anywhere under apps/backend/src) and no chat feature, so no
          equivalent unbounded-buffer pattern was copied or reimplemented
          here either.

     Official advisory: https://tomcat.apache.org/security-10.html
     (CVE-2026-66299, "Low: DoS in WebSocket chat example",
     affected 10.1.24-10.1.57, fixed 10.1.58).

     Owner: Mert Aydoğan (aydgnme). Reviewed: 2026-08-25.
     Remove this suppression immediately once tomcat.version in
     apps/backend/pom.xml is upgraded to 10.1.58 or newer — at that point
     dependency-check will stop reporting this CVE on its own, and an unused
     suppression entry only hides that the upgrade actually happened.
     Re-review, or remove, no later than the `until` date below regardless.

     Two entries, not one: two consecutive real scans against verified NVD
     data attributed CVE-2026-66299 to a DIFFERENT single tomcat-embed-*
     artifact each time — run 32856766452 to tomcat-embed-websocket only,
     run 32858443908 (minutes later, same dependency tree, same NVD data
     freshly re-verified) to tomcat-embed-core only. Dependency-Check's own
     CPE analyzer is evidently non-deterministic about which one of the two
     it reports on a given run, not that only one is genuinely affected —
     both are therefore suppressed explicitly, rather than chasing whichever
     one a given run happens to name. tomcat-embed-el has never shown this
     finding in any real run; not suppressed, since there is no observed
     finding to suppress and adding one anyway would be exactly the kind of
     speculative, unevidenced suppression this file's own rules forbid.

     No new applicability evidence was needed for either: the same
     three-point proof above already unzip -l'd and checked all three
     tomcat-embed-* jars (core, websocket, el) directly, and this
     application still packages no standalone Tomcat distribution and
     implements no WebSocket endpoints of its own either way.
   -->
   <suppress until="2026-09-30">
      <notes>
         CVE-2026-66299: low-severity DoS in Tomcat's bundled WebSocket chat
         EXAMPLE application (webapps/examples/websocket/chat), not present
         in the tomcat-embed-* JARs this Spring Boot application actually
         uses, and this application packages no standalone Tomcat
         distribution and implements no WebSocket endpoints of its own.
         Fix version 10.1.58 is not yet released. Reviewed by Mert Aydoğan
         (aydgnme), 2026-08-25. Remove on upgrading to Tomcat >= 10.1.58.
      </notes>
      <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.tomcat\.embed/tomcat\-embed\-core@10\.1\.57(\?.*)?$</packageUrl>
      <cve>CVE-2026-66299</cve>
   </suppress>
   <suppress until="2026-09-30">
      <notes>
         CVE-2026-66299: low-severity DoS in Tomcat's bundled WebSocket chat
         EXAMPLE application (webapps/examples/websocket/chat), not present
         in the tomcat-embed-* JARs this Spring Boot application actually
         uses, and this application packages no standalone Tomcat
         distribution and implements no WebSocket endpoints of its own.
         Fix version 10.1.58 is not yet released. Reviewed by Mert Aydoğan
         (aydgnme), 2026-08-25. Remove on upgrading to Tomcat >= 10.1.58.
      </notes>
      <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.apache\.tomcat\.embed/tomcat\-embed\-websocket@10\.1\.57(\?.*)?$</packageUrl>
      <cve>CVE-2026-66299</cve>
   </suppress>
</suppressions>
